Osgood-Schlatter Disease (Acute Exacerbation)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Adolescent anterior knee pain, Prominent tibial tuberosity, Exacerbation with jumping

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of anterior knee pain in an adolescent following sporting activity, resulting in an inability to weight-bear or sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (13 years old) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an adolescent experiencing severe, unrelenting knee pain and an inability to walk following a basketball tournament. Urgent clinical assessment was strictly required to rule out an acute tibial tuberosity avulsion fracture, septic arthritis, or slipped capital femoral epiphysis (presenting as knee pain), and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and analgesia prior to the next available daytime consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee hurts so much right on the bump below the kneecap; I can't even bend it to walk up the stairs after my game today."
History of Present Illness: A 13-year-old patient presents with an acute, severe flare of right anterior knee pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of mild discomfort localized strictly to the tibial tuberosity over the past 3 months during a rapid growth spurt. Today, the patient participated in a rigorous basketball tournament. Following the final game, the patient experienced a marked, severe exacerbation of pain, rendering them unable to walk without a severe limp. Reports extreme pain during running, jumping, or deep squatting activities. The pain is characterized as a deep, burning ache that is currently preventing the patient from sleeping.
Functional Status: Ambulation is severely impaired; patient requires physical support from parents to move around the house.
Social/Safety Concerns: Parental anxiety regarding potential permanent joint damage; sleep deprivation for the patient.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/75 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, holding the right leg perfectly straight.
Physical Exam: Right knee reveals a firm, visible, and palpable bony prominence at the tibial tuberosity. Exquisite, severe point tenderness is elicited upon direct, light palpation of this specific prominence. Crucially, the knee joint itself lacks any intra-articular effusion (swelling is strictly extra-articular over the tuberosity). Pain is consistently and severely reproduced by resisted active knee extension. Passive range of motion is full, but deep flexion causes tension pain at the tuberosity. Significant tightness of the quadriceps and hamstring musculature is noted. Hip examination (internal/external rotation) is completely normal and painless, ruling out referred hip pathology.
Environmental Observations: Basketball gear visible in the home, confirming the high-impact mechanical overload mechanism.
Medication Reconciliation: Parents administered Paracetamol 500mg two hours ago with minimal relief of the localized mechanical pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute severe exacerbation of Osgood-Schlatter Disease (Tibial Apophysitis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tibial Tuberosity Avulsion Fracture - Ruled out clinically by the ability to perform a straight leg raise against gravity (intact extensor mechanism), absence of a sudden catastrophic snap during a single event, and the chronic, progressive history.

Septic Arthritis of the Knee - Ruled out by the strictly extra-articular nature of the swelling, entirely normal and painless passive joint range of motion, and absence of systemic fever.

Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE) - Ruled out definitively by the completely normal, painless examination of the ipsilateral hip joint and the highly localized point tenderness at the knee.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The adolescent patient presented with a textbook, severe flare of tibial apophysitis (Osgood-Schlatter) following rigorous, high-impact athletic loading. The critical clinical imperative was to differentiate this benign, albeit intensely painful, overuse syndrome from an acute structural avulsion fracture or referred pain from a catastrophic hip pathology (SCFE). Clinical examination definitively localized the pathology to the extra-articular tibial insertion, confirmed an intact extensor mechanism, and ruled out hip joint involvement. Postponing evaluation would have subjected the child to unmanaged, severe pain, sleep deprivation, and extreme parental anxiety. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic reassurance, ruled out surgical emergencies, and initiated aggressive conservative pain and load management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food for 3-5 days to aggressively target the localized apophyseal inflammation. Advised absolute cessation of all running, jumping, and high-impact sports immediately.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly apply an ice pack wrapped in a towel directly to the bony bump for 15 minutes after any necessary walking. Consequence: Immediate suppression of the local inflammatory chemical response, drastically reducing the severe, burning pain and visibly shrinking the soft tissue swelling (positive reinforcement).
Action: Perform slow, sustained quadriceps and hamstring stretches twice daily once the acute sharp pain subsides. Consequence: Lengthening of the tight thigh muscles, taking the mechanical tension off the fragile growth plate and preventing future locking pain flares (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral highly recommended for biomechanical assessment and guided stretching protocols.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided temporary use of crutches strictly for 24-48 hours to offload the leg until the acute inflammatory crisis settles.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks. Return to sport is strictly contraindicated until completely pain-free with daily activities.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the child becomes completely unable to lift the leg straight off the bed (extensor failure), if a high fever develops, or if the knee joint itself becomes massively swollen and red.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical assessment of growth plate inflammation. X-rays are generally unnecessary unless a fracture is suspected, but may be arranged by the GP to confirm the diagnosis. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This condition is self-limiting and will completely resolve once the growth plates fuse (around age 15-16). However, the bony bump will likely remain permanently. Management relies entirely on activity modification.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your child suddenly cannot lift their leg straight up in the air at all, or if they develop a fever and the whole knee becomes hot and red.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: During a growth spurt, the bones grow faster than the muscles. Your child's tight thigh muscles are pulling aggressively on the relatively soft growth plate right below the kneecap. The jumping today caused severe microscopic tearing and inflammation at that attachment site. The crutches and anti-inflammatories will calm the acute fire, but rest is the only cure.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no basketball or running. Use the crutches for the next two days to completely rest the joint. Ice the bony bump religiously to stop the throbbing pain so your child can sleep tonight.

